# Phantasia and Aisthesis in Aristotle's De Anima 

## 1. Phantasia and Aisthesis Aristotle's account of phantasia in the *De Anima* is grounded in the prior operation of sense-perception (aisthesis), yet it cannot be reduced to perception itself; indeed, phantasia emerges as a distinct capacity of the soul that both depends upon and contrasts with the direct apprehension of sensible objects. As Aristotle makes clear in his psychological writings, perception operates through a medium that is "affected and moved by the perceptible object, and the animal by the medium" (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, pp. 53–54). Phantasia, by contrast, does not require the immediate presence of the sensible object; it produces appearances (phantasmata) that persist after the object has departed, thus functioning as a kind of residual or derivative motion within the soul. The distinction is critical because, whereas aisthesis is constrained by the actuality of the sensed object and the reliability of the sense organ, phantasia can generate images that are false—a feature that sets it apart from the veridical character that Aristotle typically ascribes to the proper objects of perception. Gonzalez underscores this point by noting the way in which phantasia operates in rhetorical contexts, where "the phantasma is what others say about us; and that the persuasion operative in the phantasia is enhanced in proportion as the speakers are trustworthy" (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 27–28). This observation reveals that phantasia is not merely a passive afterimage of sensation but can be shaped by linguistic and social inputs, a feature that opens the door to its rhetorical deployment. We Aristotle distinguishes internal grades of phantasia—sensory phantasia, which all animals share, and deliberative phantasia, which belongs to rational agents capable of comparing and weighing multiple images. The relationship between phantasia and belief (doxa) is similarly complex: phantasia does not entail belief, for one may entertain an image without assenting to it, whereas doxa involves a commitment to truth or falsity. Furthermore, phantasia possesses a degree of voluntariness absent from perception; the user is able to summon images at will, as when one "places things before one's eyes" in rhetorical practice. A fuller treatment of these internal distinctions—specifically the grades of phantasia, its relation to doxa, and the question of its voluntariness—will be developed in subsequent sections on phantasia and doxa and phantasia and pathos. 

## 2. Aisthesis and Kinesis The relationship between aisthesis and kinesis in Aristotle's psychology is foundational, for perception is itself understood as a kind of motion—a qualitative alteration (alloiōsis) that the sense organ undergoes upon receiving the form of the sensible object without its matter. Aristotle insists that perception cannot occur without a physical medium; specifically, the body must serve as "the medium for the faculty of touch, naturally attached to us, through which the several perceptions are transmitted" (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, pp. 35–36). Thus, aisthesis is not a disembodied event but is always mediated by the material conditions of the sensing body and its surrounding environment. The kinetic dimension of perception is what allows Aristotle to connect aisthesis to the broader framework of natural philosophy, where motion (kinesis) is the defining feature of the physical world. Indeed, Aristotle's treatment of mathematical and physical objects in the *Physics* clarifies that "flesh and bone and man—these are defined like snub nose, not like curved," thereby indicating that natural entities cannot be understood apart from motion (Aristotle, *Physics*, p. 19). Perception, accordingly, belongs to the domain of entities that are inseparable from motion. This kinetic account is further illuminated by Gibson's ecological approach to perception, which, while historically distant from Aristotle, shares a similar concern with the relational and event-based character of perceptual experience. Gibson insists that perception must be understood in terms of events that are "external," distinguishing the displacement of the point of observation from "the motion of a surface" (Gibson, *The Ecological Approach to Visual Perception*, pp. 116–117). Similarly, for Aristotle, perception is not a static state but an ongoing process of kinetic interaction between organism and environment, one that subsequently gives rise to the residual motions that constitute phantasia. The kinesis involved in aisthesis is thus the very ground upon which the higher psychological capacities—including phantasia, memory, and thought—are built, and it is the qualitative character of this motion that determines the fidelity of the resulting phantasma. 

## 3. Aisthesis and Dynamis We must also consider aisthesis as a dynamis—a capacity or potentiality that the soul possesses even when it is not actively perceiving. Aristotle famously draws the distinction between two senses of "that whereby we live and perceive," noting that it may mean "either knowledge or the soul, for we can speak of knowing by either, and similarly that whereby we are in health may be either health or the body or some part of the body" (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, pp. 21–22). Here, perception is treated as analogous to knowledge: just as knowledge exists both as a settled disposition (hexis) and as an active exercise (energeia), so too does perception exist as a capacity that is actualized only in the presence of the appropriate sensible object. The dynamis of aisthesis is thus not a mere absence of activity but a structured readiness, a potentiality that is oriented toward specific kinds of sensible forms. This understanding of aisthesis as dynamis has implications for the emergent character of mental life more broadly. emergent properties arise when "a neurophysiological system S comes to have a certain kind of complex physical configuration P* at time t0" such that "the baseline emergent state E is the direct result at t1" (O'Connor and Wong, *The Metaphysics of Emergence*, pp. 7–8). Similarly, for Aristotle, the dynamis of perception is realized only when the appropriate material configuration obtains—when the sense organ is properly disposed and the sensible object is present through a suitable medium. Hence, aisthesis as dynamis should be understood not as a static property but as an emergent capacity that depends on the complex organization of living matter; it is, in Aristotle's terms, the first actuality of a body that has life potentially, and it becomes second actuality only in the event of perceiving. Rickert reinforces this point in his discussion of embodied cognition, arguing that mind is not confined within the organism but involves "a mobile series of mergers and coalitions" that blur boundaries between inner and outer (Rickert, *Ambient Rhetoric*, pp. 57–60). Accordingly, the dynamis of aisthesis is always already situated within an environment, a feature that connects Aristotle's account to contemporary ecological and ambient approaches. 

## 4. Aisthesis and Apoleipomene Kinesis Aristotle's account of phantasia as a residual motion (apoleipomene kinesis) left behind by the act of perception constitutes one of the most important bridges between aisthesis and the higher cognitive faculties. The idea is that every act of perception sets in motion a kinetic trace within the soul, and this trace persists even after the sensible object has been removed. Aristotle makes clear that the soul discriminates between sensible qualities by means of a unified yet divisible faculty, observing that "that which discriminates is qua undivided one, and active in a single moment of time, while qua divisible it twice over uses the same dot at one and the same time" (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, pp. 40–42). This capacity for simultaneous the perceptual act generates complex, structured residua—not merely isolated afterimages but organized kinetic patterns that can subsequently be reactivated in phantasia. The notion of apoleipomene kinesis thus provides the material substrate for phantasia: it is the motion "left behind" by perception that allows the soul to entertain images in the absence of the original sensible object. This residual character explains both the dependence of phantasia on aisthesis and its relative independence, for while the phantasma is causally derived from perception, it can be activated, combined, and manipulated in ways that perception itself cannot accomplish. Thus, the apoleipomene kinesis serves as the hinge between the passive receptivity of aisthesis and the active image-making of phantasia. Indeed, Aristotle's account in the *Movement of Animals* confirms this connection between perception, imagination, and desire: "when a man is actually using perception or imagination or thought in relation to that for the sake of which, what he desires he does at once" (Aristotle, *Movement Of Animals*, p. 8). Here, the residual motion of phantasia is integrated into the practical syllogism, demonstrating that the kinetic trace left by perception is not inert but dynamically available for deliberation and action. 

## 5. Phantasma and Phantasia The relationship between phantasma and phantasia requires careful delineation, for the two terms, though etymologically linked, designate different aspects of the image-making capacity of the soul. Phantasia refers to the faculty or process by which images are produced, while phantasma denotes the specific image or appearance that results from that process. Aristotle's account makes clear that phantasmata are the immediate products of the residual motions (apoleipomenai kinēseis) that persist after perception, and they serve as the raw material for memory, deliberation, and thought. Gonzalez draws attention to the rhetorical significance of this relationship, noting that in certain passages "the phantasma is what others say about us; and that the persuasion operative in the phantasia is enhanced in proportion as the speakers are trustworthy" (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 27–28). This observation is crucial because it reveals that phantasmata are not exclusively sensory residua; they can also be generated by verbal reports and social interactions, a point that expands the scope of phantasia beyond the narrowly perceptual. Specifically, the distinction between sensory and deliberative phantasia is operative here: sensory phantasia produces phantasmata that closely replicate the original perceptual experience, while deliberative phantasia combines and rearranges these images in the service of practical reasoning. The relation of phantasma to doxa is similarly nuanced; one may hold a phantasma before the mind without thereby forming a belief, for phantasia does not by itself entail assent. Furthermore, phantasmata possess a degree of voluntariness that distinguishes them from percepts—the user is able to summon, dismiss, or recombine images at will, a capacity that Hawhee connects to the rhetorical practice of producing magnitude (megethos) through the accumulation of vivid images. As Hawhee observes, epideictic speeches "take up actions that are agreed upon, so that what remains is to clothe the actions with greatness (megethos) and beauty" (Hawhee, *Looking Into Aristotle's Eyes*, pp. 12–13). The phantasma, accordingly, is both a cognitive and a rhetorical instrument, mediating between raw perception and the deliberative activity of the rational soul. 

## 6. Phantasia and Mneme Memory (mneme) is, for Aristotle, inseparable from phantasia, since every act of remembering involves the reactivation of a phantasma—a residual image originally produced by perception. The soul cannot remember without recalling an image, and thus phantasia serves as the necessary condition for the preservation and retrieval of past experience. Aristotle's insistence that perception operates through media and produces structured kinetic residua entails that memory is itself a kinetic phenomenon; it is the re-enlivening of a prior motion within the soul. In this context, Aristotle's observation that the soul discriminates qualities through a faculty that is "a sort of unity, but in the way a boundary is" (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, pp. 47–48) is significant, for the phantasmata available for memory are not isolated fragments but are organized in relational structures that preserve the qualitative distinctions of the original perceptual experience. Gonzalez's analysis of phantasia in the rhetorical tradition social and communicative dimensions of memory: the phantasma can be constituted by what others report, and the persuasive force of the phantasia is amplified by the trustworthiness of the source (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 27–28). Subsequently, the connection between phantasia and mneme is not merely psychological but rhetorical: the orator who furnishes vivid images enables the audience to remember, and memory in turn sustains the persuasive effect over time. The internal distinctions within phantasia—its sensory and deliberative grades, its complex relation to doxa, and its voluntariness—are all relevant to memory, for the character of the remembered image depends on whether it was produced by direct sensory experience, by deliberative recombination, or by the testimony of others. These distinctions will be further refined in our discussion of phantasia and doxa below. 

## 7. Phantasia and Doxa The relationship between phantasia and doxa (belief or opinion) is one of the most carefully drawn distinctions in Aristotle's psychology, for while both faculties involve the representation of objects, they differ crucially in their commitment to truth. Doxa entails a judgment—an assertion that something is or is not the case—whereas phantasia merely presents an appearance without requiring assent. Aristotle clarifies this difference by insisting that phantasia can produce false images without thereby generating false beliefs; hence, phantasia and doxa are not identical, even if they frequently co-occur. Heidegger's reading of Aristotle's account of the passions illuminates this point from a different angle: as the authors of *Heidegger and Rhetoric* note, "Passion itself is both motion and a cause of motions; it is a capacity for altering" (Multiple Authors, *Heidegger and Rhetoric*, p. 115). This characterization of passion as kinetic alteration resonates with the kinetic character of phantasia, which, like pathos, produces changes in the soul without necessarily engaging the deliberative assent that characterizes doxa. Specifically, the voluntariness of phantasia marks another point of contrast with doxa: while beliefs are typically formed through processes of reasoning and persuasion that involve a commitment to evidence, phantasmata can be summoned or entertained at will, independently of any evidential warrant. The grades of phantasia are similarly relevant here, for deliberative phantasia—the capacity to combine and compare images in the service of practical reasoning—comes closer to doxa than does sensory phantasia, which merely replicates the content of past perception. Thus, Aristotle's psychology establishes a spectrum of representational capacities, from the involuntary reception of sensory forms in aisthesis, through the voluntary production and manipulation of images in phantasia, to the judgmental commitment of doxa, each presupposing the one before it in a layered architecture of the soul. 

## 8. Phantasia and Pathos The intimate connection between phantasia and pathos (passion or emotion) is central to Aristotle's account of both psychology and rhetoric, for the passions are aroused not merely by direct perception but by the images that phantasia places before the soul. Aristotle's treatment of animal motion confirms this linkage: "desire arises through perception or through imagination and thought" (Aristotle, *Movement Of Animals*, p. 8). Hence, pathos is never a brute physiological event but is always mediated by the representational activity of phantasia; the soul is moved to anger, fear, or pity because it entertains a phantasma of the relevant situation. The Heideggerian reading further enriches this account, for Heidegger interprets Aristotle's pathe not as static knowledge but as Befindlichkeiten—modes of attunement that disclose the world in affectively charged ways. As the commentary in *Heidegger and Rhetoric* observes, passions "alter judgements," and it is precisely at this juncture that "passions intrude on logos" (Multiple Authors, *Heidegger and Rhetoric*, p. 115). The implications for rhetoric are profound: the orator who produces vivid phantasmata in the audience thereby arouses passions and, through those passions, alters judgment. This is why Aristotle connects lexis (style) so closely with phantasia in *Rhetoric* III.1, for style is the instrument by which the speaker furnishes the audience with images that engage their affective capacities. Gonzalez's observation that the persuasion operative in phantasia is enhanced by the trustworthiness of the speakers (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 27–28) confirms that pathos is not separable from the social and epistemic conditions of phantasia. The grades of phantasia—sensory and deliberative—are both relevant to the arousal of pathos: sensory phantasia furnishes vivid, immediate images that provoke strong affective responses, while deliberative phantasia enables the comparative weighing of images that modulates emotional intensity. The voluntariness of phantasia is similarly crucial, for it is precisely because images can be summoned and arranged at will that the rhetor is able to craft speeches designed to produce specific emotional effects. Accordingly, the relationship between phantasia and pathos reveals the deep interconnection of cognitive, affective, and rhetorical dimensions in Aristotle's philosophical psychology. --- ```

